  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 AM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nRain continues to meander over the state this morning. Rain should \nquickly dry out and end by late morning or early afternoon as the \nlow pressure system over the northeast pushes even further northeast \ntoday. A brief period of ridging will settle in for today and \ntomorrow, allowing temperatures to warm some. Highs today will be \nin the mid 40s and low 50s tomorrow. \n\nThe next chance for substantial rain comes Saturday night into \nSunday as a shortwave digs into the Plains from the northern \nRockies. A low pressure system will develop out of the shortwave \nover Oklahoma, with the low tracking across southern IL on Sunday. \nWith temperatures in the upper 30s to upper 40s for the duration of \nthe system, rain is the expected precipitation type. PWATs look to \nbe around 0.75-1.00 inches. This widespread rain event has a 25-50% \nchance of exceeding 0.5 inches of rain east of I-155, based off of \nLREF 24 hour precipitation probabilities. The NBM statistics show \nhigher QPF amounts, with the mean QPF being 0.6-0.9 inches. \n\nBeyond Saturday night's system, a high pressure center builds over \nthe central part of the United States. This will generate a gradual \nwarming trend into the new week. By Thursday, highs will be in the \nlow 60s, with a 40-60% chance of highs exceeding 60 degrees from the \nNBM. Increased moisture return is depicted by the synoptic models \nlate next week. The GFS and Euro are showing a late week system \nmoving through with thunderstorms possible. 60+ temperatures in \nDecember could be a bad omen for unsettled (or even severe) weather. \nAs of now, the models aren't showing any significant amount of CAPE, \nbut bulk wind shear of 50-60+ knots. As this is still a week out, \nfuture forecasts will monitor trends as things evolve.",
